---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import VerificationExecutiveSummary from "../components/diagrams/VerificationExecutiveSummary.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Verification | BeaconGuard Verify"
  description="BeaconGuard Verify is a read-only investigation surface for executive reporting, Decision Explorer, deterministic reconstruction, source evidence, replay verification, verifiable export, and non-authoritative AI-assisted insights."
>
  <section class="hero-split hero-split--verify">
    <div class="hero-split-copy">
      <p class="hero-eyebrow">BEACONGUARD VERIFY</p>
      <h1>Investigate decisions without changing authority.</h1>
      <p class="hero-subheadline">
        BeaconGuard Verify gives security, privacy, compliance, risk, and audit
        reviewers a read-only surface for inspecting governed AI transaction
        evidence without granting runtime permission.
      </p>
      <div class="page-actions page-actions--hero page-actions--hero-split">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence">Inspect Proof and Evidence</a>
        <a class="btn-secondary" href="/sample-decision-record">View Sample Decision Record</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-split-visual">
      <VerificationExecutiveSummary />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Why the verification surface exists</h2>
    <p>
      Authorization decisions must be reconstructable after the fact. Verify
      provides investigation, reporting, and export capabilities over canonical
      evidence while remaining outside the runtime enforcement path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Read-only trust boundary</h2>
    <p>
      BeaconGuard Verify is read-only. It cannot modify evidence, change a
      historical decision, activate a release, alter a Compliance Playbook,
      grant runtime authority, or authorize an enterprise action.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Verification does not participate in ALLOW or DENY.</li>
      <li>Browser-side claims are untrusted for integrity conclusions.</li>
      <li>Export signing and reconstruction remain server-side responsibilities.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Executive Reporting</h2>
    <p>
      Deterministic reporting summarizes investigation-relevant decision
      evidence for review. Report views are for analysis and drill-down, not for
      granting runtime authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Decision Explorer</h2>
    <p>
      Decision Explorer supports inspection of recorded authorization decisions
      and linked investigation context for a governed transaction.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Deterministic Reconstruction</h2>
    <p>
      Reconstruction derives the logical decision path from canonical evidence.
      Missing or unavailable fields are reported as such. Reconstruction does
      not invent model reasoning or fabricate missing artifacts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Source Evidence and field traceability</h2>
    <p>
      Source Evidence maps reconstructed fields back to underlying artifacts,
      digests, and integrity status so reviewers can see what was verified,
      unverified, invalid, or unavailable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Replay Verification</h2>
    <p>
      Investigation replay verification checks recorded decisions against
      governing artifacts. It is not a substitute for live provider calls and
      is distinct from request-admission replay protection at runtime.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Verifiable Export</h2>
    <p>
      Verifiable Export produces investigation packages that can be checked for
      integrity. Export material supports review workflows; it is not claimed
      here as legal admissibility.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>AI-Assisted Insights</h2>
    <p>
      AI-Assisted Insights may help reviewers navigate or summarize, but they
      are non-authoritative. Insights must not enter the trusted reconstruction
      path, the evidence integrity path, or the runtime authorization path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>What Verify cannot do</h2>
    <ul class="bg-nonclaim-list">
      <li>Modify or delete historical evidence.</li>
      <li>Convert DENY into ALLOW.</li>
      <li>Activate or replace an Active Verified Release.</li>
      <li>Alter a Compliance Playbook.</li>
      <li>Grant runtime authority or authorize an enterprise action.</li>
      <li>Treat AI-assisted insight output as evidence authority.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Responsibility separation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>BeaconGuard records and exposes investigation evidence.</li>
      <li>Enterprise applications execute only authorized actions.</li>
      <li>Systems of record remain authoritative for business state.</li>
      <li>Customer governance retains clinical, legal, and compliance judgment.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/proof-evidence">Continue to Proof and Evidence</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/reviewer-kit">Open Reviewer Kit</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
